    <notes>Oxyrhynchus Hymn
The Christian hymn of P.Oxy. XV 1786: the earliest Christian hymn with musical
notation, and one of the last pieces written down in the ancient Greek
notation. Source: M. L. West, Ancient Greek Music (Oxford 1992), no. 51,
pp. 324-325. The papyrus was copied in the later 3rd c. AD at Oxyrhynchus,
Egypt, and is kept in Oxford. The notation is Hypolydian and purely diatonic,
with a compass of one octave.

...ytaneo sigato, med' astra phaesphora lampesthon, ... potamon rhothion
pasai; hymnounton d' hemon patera k'hyion k'hagion pneuma, pasai dynameis
epiphonounton: Amen, amen. Kratos, ainos aei kai doxa theoi doteri monoi
panton agathon. Amen, amen.
In sense: let all be silent, let the light-bearing stars not shine, let the
rushing rivers be still; while we sing of Father, Son and Holy Spirit, let all
the powers answer "Amen, amen". Might, praise and glory for ever to God, the
only giver of all good things. Amen, amen.

Pitch: West prints the melody a tone below the notated pitch, with final d.
Here it is a further tone lower, with final C, so that it lies on the same
scale as the Seikilos song: kitmum, the Mixolydian mode on C of the Pythagorean
gamut, C D E F G A B-flat = 1/1 9/8 81/64 4/3 3/2 27/16 16/9. In 53-EDO's
notation the Pythagorean E, A and B-flat are written E-up, A-up and
B-flat-down. The melody runs from B-flat to B-flat and rests on C and F.

Rhythm: anapaestic, a long syllable being a quarter and a short an eighth,
with the papyrus's own rests before "sigato" and "hymnounton" (bars 4 and 9)
and after "pneuma" (bar 12). One row is a sixteenth.

Gaps: only the last part of the hymn survives, and West's transcription has
seven lost notes inside it and one break. Nothing is invented. Each lost note
is marked ??? at its row on the four melody tracks: row 32 (first note), rows
84 and 86 (bar 6, "lam-"), row 92 (bar 6, "-thon"), row 152 (bar 10, "pa-")
and rows 250 and 254 (bar 16, "a-ei"). The break before "potamon" is marked
~~~ at row 96 (bar 7); West leaves half a bar for it, but its real length is
unknown. The drones and drum carry on through all of them.

The papyrus gives pitch and rhythm only. Our realisation choices: the tempo (a
quarter at 76); the aulos (double reed-pipe, here an oboe) carrying the sung
line in place of the voice, two pipes, the second a few milliseconds late; the
lyre doubling it, with a second lyre an octave below; a lyre drone on C and G
at each bar and a low drone pipe on C; a frame drum (tympanon) on each bar;
small cymbals (kymbala) where a new clause begins, on each Amen and on the
final chord; a two-bar anabole in which the lyre runs through its open strings
and settles on the fifth; and a two-bar outro that echoes the cadence and
settles on C and G while the tempo broadens from 76 to 64.</notes>
